.\" Man page for git-edit. Kept in sync with the script's USAGE() and README.
.\" Auto-found via man's PATH derivation (<repo>/man/man1) once the repo is on
.\" PATH, so `git edit --help` / `man git-edit` work with no install step.
.TH GIT-EDIT 1 "2026-10-04" "git-edit" "Git Extensions"
.SH NAME
git\-edit \- edit, reword, drop, squash, reorder, split, or fold past commits
.PP
Reading this without a terminal? \fBgit edit \-h\fR prints the usage directly.
.B \-\-help
reaches this page through
.BR man ,
which then emits backspace\-overstruck text.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-e\fR | \fB\-\-edit\fR] \fIcommit\fR               # edit a commit (default)
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-M\fR | \fB\-\-reword\fR] [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR | \fB\-\-subject\fR \fIline\fR] \fIcommit\fR
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-s\fR | \fB\-\-squash\fR | \fB\-d\fR | \fB\-\-drop\fR] \fIcommit\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-s=\fR\fItarget\fR] \fIcommit\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR | \fBauto\fR} [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR] [\fB\-\-tree=\fR\fItree\-ish\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-split=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-reorder\fR \fIcommit\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-move=\fR{\fIsha\fR | \fIoldest\fR\fB..\fR\fInewest\fR}... {\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR}
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-onto=\fR\fIupstream\fR                    # replant this branch onto \fIupstream\fR
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-land=\fR\fIbranch\fR [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]   # land \fIbranch\fR's commits on this branch
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-exec\fR [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-commit\fR \fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] [\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR | \fBauto\fR} \fB\-\-whole\fR [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR {\fB\-\-commit \-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR [\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR] | \fB\-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR | \fBauto\fR}} [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] {\fB\-\-edits=\fR\fIjson\fR | \fB\-\-patch=\fR\fIfile\fR | \fB\-\-put=\fR\fIpath\fR\fB=\fR\fIfile\fR | \fB\-\-rm=\fR\fIpath\fR | \fB\-\-chmod=\fR\fIpath\fR\fB=\fR{\fB+x\fR|\fB\-x\fR}}... [[\fB\-\-whole\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-commit\fR \fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR \fB\-\-tree=\fR\fIcommit\fR [\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-continue\fR | \fB\-\-skip\fR | \fB\-\-abort\fR | \fB\-\-undo\fR | \fB\-\-status\fR | \fB\-\-carry\fR[\fB=\fR\fIsha\fR] | \fB\-\-selftest\fR[\fB=\fR\fIids\fR] [\fB\-\-jobs\fR[\fB=\fR\fIn\fR]] | \fB\-\-version\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git edit
is a convenience wrapper around
.BR git\-rebase (1)
for editing, reordering, dropping, merging, splitting, and folding earlier
commits. It is hardened for use by AI coding agents and parallel sessions:
.IP \(bu 3
Every ref move is an atomic compare\-and\-swap (3\-arg
.BR "git update\-ref" ),
so a concurrent commit from another worktree or session is refused, never
overwritten.
.IP \(bu 3
The plumbing modes
.RB ( \-M ,
.BR \-S ,
a pathspec
.BR \-\-split )
and the isolated modes
.RB ( \-\-amend\-into ,
a content
.BR \-\-split ,
.BR \-\-reorder ,
.BR \-\-exec ,
.BR \-\-commit ,
.BR \-\-land )
build their result without touching the main working tree \(em only once a
run lands does it bring the checkout along \(em and folds do not
consume staged changes until the final CAS lands \(em so a failure or
.B \-\-abort
leaves your staged changes exactly as they were, with nothing to roll back.
.IP \(bu 3
An edit, drop or squash, and a land's replay, runs in a temporary worktree
of its own too, or the
.B \-C
path, reading the config the branch's checkout reads \(em an
.B onbranch
include's identity or signing key, a relative
.B core.hooksPath
whose untracked hooks only the checkout has, as husky's \(em through an
.B includeIf
only this repository's git dirs read. At a terminal \(em stdin and stdout a
TTY, with none of
.BR CI ,
.B CLAUDECODE
or
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
set \(em a pause asks rather than exits. See
.BR "TERMINAL RUNS" .
A run that lands, an agent's as a person's, brings the checkout it started in
along. See
.BR "THE CHECKOUT" .
.IP \(bu 3
Output carries blank\-line padding only when stdout is a terminal. Piped or
captured it comes out tight \(em same lines, no padding \(em so even a
.B "| tail -3"
reaches the trailer and the line naming the commit the run touched \(em every
mode but \fB\-\-exec\fR, whose command is yours and has no single target.
Nothing above those two is guaranteed: a stat block or path list grows with
the commit. Colour goes with the padding, so a captured line starts at column
zero and a name reaches further than a count \(em
.B "| grep -E '^(Verif|Tip\ tree|Trees|Note:\ tip\ tree|git-edit:)'"
answers what landed, what checked it and whether the trees came out as they
were (\fBTip tree identical\fR, \fBTrees unchanged\fR) or not (a fold's
\fBNote: tip tree differs from the staged result\fR), wherever the gate's
summary sits. A run
that had a gate and declined it reports that too \(em
.B "Verify skipped \(en --no-verify was passed, so `npm test` never ran"
\(em so silence means only that the repo configured no gate.
.IP \(bu 3
Every rewrite reports its old\(->new map the way git does: notes are carried
across it (honoring \fBnotes.rewriteRef\fR and \fBnotes.rewriteMode\fR, and
doing nothing when unset), and \fBpost\-rewrite\fR hooks run with the same
pairs on stdin \(em a hook file and one defined in config alike, through
.B git hook run
(git 2.40 and later, where older releases run the file alone). It happens
once, when the rewrite lands: the rebase\-based modes hold git's own delivery
back until then, so an aborted or refused rewrite is never announced, and a
commit the run makes along the way (a fold's \fBfixup!\fR) is never listed. A
.B git rebase \-\-continue
typed into a pause worktree that finishes the rebase lets git deliver it
there, and the landing does not repeat it. The plumbing modes compose commits with \fBcommit\-tree\fR
rather than replaying them, so without this they would orphan anything keyed
to a SHA. A squash maps every member onto the commit that absorbed them; a
split maps the original onto the remainder, which keeps its message and tree.
.IP \(bu 3
Only the branch being rewritten moves. Another branch or a tag left pointing
into the rewritten span is named instead, with the command that would re\-point
it to its counterpart \(em the commit the rewrite's own record maps it to, said
so where its change differs, a split's remainder or a fold's target say, else
matched the way a stale \fIcommit\fR is, so only a commit carrying the same
change is offered as one, and a subject match alone is offered as a guess to
check first. One on a commit the run dropped is named as such, with no
command, and one on a commit it squashed away names the commit it went into.
.B rebase.updateRefs
is not applied: git moves such a branch as its rebase completes, before the
result is verified or lands, so an aborted or refused rewrite would leave the
branch rewritten. A branch checked out in a worktree is left for that worktree
to move, as git leaves it too \(em but it is named all the same, with its
counterpart and no command, since the move belongs to that checkout and to
whatever work is sitting in it.
.IP \(bu 3
A \fIcommit\fR that an earlier rewrite has orphaned is resolved to its current
identity when one commit carries the identical diff, and otherwise refused with
its likely counterpart named \(em so a stale SHA never silently no\-ops. A match
that is itself pushed is named but never substituted, not even under
.BR \-\-allow\-pushed ,
which consents to rewriting the commit you named rather than one inferred on
your behalf. Candidates are pruned by author date, which every rewrite
preserves, and where a single timestamp holds more than 100 commits,
uniqueness cannot be established from that partial view \(em resolution then
degrades to naming a suggestion rather than acting on one.
.IP \(bu 3
Merge topology is preserved: the plumbing modes rebuild merge commits with
their parents faithfully substituted, and the rebase\-based modes refuse a
span containing a merge rather than silently flattening it.
.PP
Every run ends with a machine\-readable status trailer (see
.BR "OUTCOME TRAILER" ),
refuses to rewrite commits that already exist on a remote unless
.B \-\-allow\-pushed
is given, records its ref move in the branch reflog and a journal, and warns
about tags and branches left pointing into the rewritten span.
.PP
It needs git 2.31 or later and refuses an older one outright, since below
that some of its checks would read as off rather than fail. Every mode needs a
checked\-out branch but
.BR \-M ,
.B \-S
and a split by pathspec.
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-version
answer regardless.
.SH MODES
A mode or option that takes one value \(em
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
.BR \-\-split ,
.BR \-\-onto ,
.BR \-\-land ,
.BR \-s= ,
.BR \-\-after ,
.BR \-\-before ,
.BR \-\-tree ,
.BR \-\-text ,
.BR \-\-subject ,
.BR \-\-verify ,
.BR \-\-edits ,
.BR \-\-base ,
.BR \-C / \-\-dir ,
.BR \-\-selftest ,
.B \-\-jobs
or
.B \-\-carry
\(em refuses to be given twice, where only one value would count.
.B \-\-move
alone repeats, each time naming more of what it moves. A value given apart
that is a flag, or the
.B \-\-
ending them, refuses as a value gone missing; glue one on with
.B =
where it is meant. A long option's value is given apart or after
.BR = ,
so a word running on past an option's name, as
.BR \-\-text\-file ,
is an unknown option. A \fIcommit\fR may be
a range \fIA\fB..\fIB\fR, which includes both its ends, unlike git's own. One
end an earlier rewrite orphaned resolves as a single \fIcommit\fR does; with
both orphaned, each commit of the range as named resolves alone, as the span
between their current identities can hold a commit moved in since.
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-edit
Edit \fIcommit\fR (default). Pauses to let you change files, then amends and
replays the descendants. Naming the mode refuses a second \fIcommit\fR, which
the bare form reads as a squash. The pause holds a worktree checked out at the
commit, named by a
.B paused
trailer \(em edit files there (clean of any main\-checkout WIP), then
.B git edit \-\-continue
(optionally with
.B \-\-text
to also reword, or
.B \-\-subject
to replace the subject alone) amends, replays, prints the net history change,
and
CAS\-applies, or press Enter at a terminal's prompt. The branch never moves
before the final apply. The edit was authored in that worktree, and once it
lands the main checkout comes along as after any landing \(em see
.BR "THE CHECKOUT" .
Where it can't, the index entries the move stranded are re\-synced and the
paths still holding the pre\-edit content named, with the one
.B \-\-carry
that brings them along, never a restore. A path carrying further uncommitted
edits is named apart \(em as taking the rewrite back if committed whole
where those edits sit on the pre\-edit content, and beside its new path where
the rewrite renamed it, the edits left untracked at the old one, or as missing
where the checkout has no file there, deleted or never moved in from the old
path of an earlier run's rename \(em as are the
untracked files left in a directory it renamed whole, each with its place under
the new one, and those right in one it renamed only in part, which stay \(em
as is a
directory standing where a file lands, and a path under a symlink or a file
of the caller's; a case\-only rename on a
filesystem that ignores case reads as its new name alone \(em and one
holding the new content under another mode
\(em an executable bit lost to a file moved into place \(em is named apart,
both modes given, with the
.B chmod
that matches the commit. A command printed over more than ten paths reads
them through
.BR xargs (1)
from a file the run writes and names, so it runs at any count, and the list
beside it names ten and how many more; a list no command covers names every
path, as each needs its own action, and a name holding a newline is spelled as
\fB$\(aq\&...\(aq\fR spells it, so a list keeps to its line. A fold and
.B \-\-exec
bring the checkout along the same way. The amend stages the worktree
wholesale, so a scratch file left there joins the commit \(em any untracked file it absorbs is named before
the amend. Commits made at the stop stand in for the amend, what followed
\fIcommit\fR replaying onto them: one amending \fIcommit\fR is named as the
edited commit, while ones made on top of it are named as added after it, which
stays as it was. A worktree reset there to a commit that followed \fIcommit\fR
stands in for nothing and refuses, as does an edit that empties \fIcommit\fR
\(em that refusal names
.B \-\-abort
and then
.B \-d
to drop it instead.
.TP
.B \-M, \-\-reword
Alter only \fIcommit\fR's message, via plumbing \(em no checkout, stash, rebase,
or worktree; the working tree is untouched and the tip tree is unchanged.
Descendants of a linear history are rebuilt in one \fBgit replay\fR pass (git
2.44+), kept only once every rebuilt commit is proven to carry its original's
tree, author and message \(em otherwise, as under a merge, one
\fBcommit\-tree\fR per commit. Either way a commit only rebuilt keeps its
message and author line byte for byte, as does the message a plumbing squash
keeps, and the rebuild ends at the tip the run read. Where the branch moved
meanwhile the run refuses, naming the move, with nothing applied \(em run it
again on the new tip. A message typed in an editor for the run
.RB ( \-M ,
.B \-S \-m
or
.BR "\-s \-m" )
is kept in a file inside the repository's git directory, which the refusal
names with the
rerun that takes it through
.BR "\-\-text \-" .
.IP
Drop \fIcommit\fR and pass \fB\-\-text \-\fR to reword many commits at once,
reading \fB\-\-\-\fR \fIcommit\fR records from stdin \(em a header naming a
commit, then its new message, the shape
.B git log \-\-format='\-\-\- %h%n%B'
emits. One walk rebuilds the span under a single compare\-and\-swap rather than
one replay per commit; it stays plumbing (every tree reused verbatim), skips a
record whose message is unchanged, and is all\-or\-nothing \(em an unreachable,
pushed, empty, or duplicated target aborts the batch before anything is written.
A stale SHA resolves by patch id. A commit whose own message contains a
.B \-\-\-
line is refused (that line is the record separator) \(em reword it singly.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-drop
Delete one or more \fIcommit\fRs or ranges. The dropped content leaves the
checkout with the commit, edits on it kept, the run naming the paths it took
out with the way back \(em
.B "git edit \-\-undo"
while it is the last run, else
.BR "git show \fIold\-tip\fB:\fIpath\fR" .
Dropping every commit of the branch refuses.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-squash
Merge \fIcommit\fRs into the oldest of them (or into \fItarget\fR with
.BR \-s= \fItarget\fR),
a single \fIcommit\fR into its parent. Auto\-routes to the plumbing path
when the selection is a
contiguous, merge\-free range whose target is the oldest commit.
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-resquash
Strict squash: plumbing only; errors instead of falling back to a rebase.
.TP
.B \-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR|\fBauto\fR} [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR] [\fB\-\-tree=\fR\fItree\-ish\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
Fold the currently\-staged changes into \fIsha\fR in an isolated worktree, as a
fixup placed beside it. A \fIpathspec\fR narrows the fold to those staged paths and
leaves the rest staged \(em worth using when the index may hold more than you
mean to fold, since a parallel session can stage into it between your
.BR git\-add (1)
and this call. A \fIpathspec\fR holding pattern characters, or opening with a
.B :
that no magic follows, that names a staged
path, a staged removal included (or, with
.BR \-\-tree ,
a path the tree holds or removes), is that path alone \(em as a pattern too,
.I app/[id].tsx
would also fold
.IR app/i.tsx ,
and git reads
.I :a.txt
as
.IR a.txt ;
one naming no path stays as git reads it, and magic given outright
(\fB:(\fR...\fB)\fR, \fB:/\fR, \fB:!\fR, \fB:^\fR) stays as given.
.B auto
targets the unpushed commit that last touched the folded \fIlines\fR \(em staged here,
or composed by the inputs,
.B \-\-whole
or
.B \-\-tree
\(em (per
.BR git\-blame ),
falling back to the newest commit touching the staged paths only where the
change adds lines and so attributes to nothing. It refuses when the evidence
points at several commits, or at a pushed one, and where a submodule's pointer
is among the changes, which no line attributes \(em name its commit instead.
Only staged changes are folded;
unstaged edits are left untouched.
A conflict on the fold's final step resolves itself, as a reorder's does:
that step's result is the staged tree, provable before anything is committed,
so only earlier steps pause \(em and so does the final one when that
resolution would leave its commit empty, since whether the overlapped change
survives is the resolver's call. A pause carries any name but one holding a
newline: a fold that would pause over one refuses instead, naming it, with
nothing landed. The completion summary names the folded paths, ten at most and
how many more, and verifies what landed: it counts commits a conflict
resolution left empty (the rebase drops them), and
notes a tip that differs from the staged result \(em the pre\-op tip plus
the staged changes, which a faithful fold must reproduce. A fold that would leave history unchanged
\(em every staged hunk already carried by the target, or its fixup stepped
over by hand with
.B "git rebase \-\-skip"
\(em is refused, with the staged changes left intact.
A staged path that does not exist at \fIsha\fR is refused when a later
commit introduces it \(em folded beneath its introduction, that commit
replays as an add/add conflict of the whole file \(em with the introducing
commit named;
.B \-\-allow\-new\-path
overrides. A removal folded there is refused too, with no override, as that
later commit brings the path back.
A path renamed since folds onto its old name instead, wherever
git's rename detection pairs the two as the replay's own merge will, and a
note names the old path. Where it does not \(em later edits outgrew the old
content \(em but the commit the path arrives in renamed it, the refusal of a
path the fold keeps names
the old path at \fIsha\fR and that commit, and the edit pause that changes the
file under its old name:
.BI "git edit " sha \fR,
the change made to the old path in its worktree, then
.BR "git edit \-\-continue" .
A path new to the entire span backfills cleanly and
passes with a note. A flipped file mode is refused as well \(em a fold rarely
means one, and the usual cause is a literal mode typed into
.BR "git update\-index \-\-cacheinfo" ,
which takes the executable bit with it and would ride into history
invisibly, a diffstat counting lines only;
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
overrides. The guard holds through the replay: at a conflict stop,
.B \-\-continue
refuses a staged resolution that leaves a path at a mode neither the
replayed commit nor its new parent carries \(em the way a file written anew
into the worktree and staged comes back, without its executable bit \(em and
names the
.B chmod
and
.B git add
that restore it, worktree and index both; and the landing refuses a tip carrying a mode change
nothing asked for, naming the rebuilt commit that brought it in.
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
on the continue keeps either.
.IP
.B \-\-tree
takes the fold's content from \fItree\-ish\fR instead of the index \(em for
the checkout whose shared index is itself the hazard, a parallel session's
uncommitted edits sitting in the very file. Compose it apart from that index:
seed a private one from
.B HEAD
.RB ( "GIT_INDEX_FILE=... git read\-tree HEAD" ),
place the blobs at
.BR HEAD 's
modes, and
.BR write\-tree .
Handed over as a commit on
.BR HEAD ,
it pins the tip it was composed on, and a tip that moved since is refused
rather than have the diff carry a newer commit's reversal; a bare tree
carries no such pin, and is checked against other callers' landings instead.
The shared index is neither read nor written \(em it comes along afterwards
with the checkout as after any landing, an entry another caller staged kept.
.IP
A fold of what is staged is guarded as
.B \-\-commit
is, reading the staging once for the guard and the fold alike: staged before
another caller's landing, it would take that landing back where the fold
rebuilds those files as staged, so it refuses, naming the
.B "git restore \-\-staged"
that takes what landed instead, or the
.B \-\-carry
that merges what landed into the checkout and the
.B "git add"
that stages the files again \(em a file the landing renamed unstaged first,
as a carry leaves a staged old path where it is, and staged again under its
new name. That
.B "git add"
is named only for a file the checkout holds just as staged; one holding more,
another caller's uncommitted lines say, has its staging rebuilt alone instead,
what landed merged into the staged copy by the
.B git merge\-file
and
.B "git update\-index \-\-cacheinfo"
named, under the entry's own mode, and one whose staging conflicts with what
landed is to be replayed with
.B \-\-edits
or
.BR \-\-patch ,
then unstaged. For an executable bit alone, it names the
.B "git update\-index \-\-chmod"
that sets it on the staging, which staging the file again would not.
Staging or a
.B \-\-tree
carrying conflict markers its tip lacks refuses as a file taken whole does,
and a
.B \-\-tree
spelling a name two ways a case\-insensitive filesystem reads as one refuses as
.B \-\-commit
does.
.IP
.B \-\-text
also rewords \fIsha\fR in the same run \(em
.BR git\-commit (1)
.BR \-\-amend " " \-m
semantics. The reword is plumbing and runs before the fixup, so both land
under one ref update and neither can be left applied without the other.
.TP
.B \-\-split=\fIsha\fR [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
Split \fIsha\fR into two commits: the \fIpathspec\fR\-matched changes are
extracted into their own commit (message from
.BR \-\-text ),
the rest keep the original message. Plumbing only \(em no conflicts possible.
The \fIpathspec\fR has to match a nonempty, proper subset of the commit's
changes: matching none of them, or all of them, is refused rather than
composing an empty half. One holding pattern characters, or opening with a
.B :
that no magic follows, that names a path in
the commit is that path alone, as under
.BR \-\-amend\-into .
Where the commit turns a file into a directory at one path, the
\fIpathspec\fR names both sides or neither \(em one taking the directory's
files without the file they replace is refused, naming it. A \fIsha\fR an
earlier rewrite staled resolves to its current identity, as in every other mode.
.IP
With no \fIpathspec\fR \(em for halves that share a file, which no pathspec can
name apart \(em the split pauses instead, with an isolated worktree holding the
commit's own content. Edit it back to what the first commit should leave
behind, then
.B git edit \-\-continue
(with
.B \-\-text
if it was not given up front) commits that tree, re\-commits \fIsha\fR's tree on
top, and replays the descendants. It refuses rather than committing nonsense
when either half would be empty, when the worktree touches a path \fIsha\fR
never did, when it carries a file mode neither \fIsha\fR nor its parent does
(a file written anew into the worktree comes back without its executable bit,
and the remainder would flip it straight back \(em
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
keeps it), or when \fIsha\fR was rewritten mid\-pause.
.IP
Both forms insert a new commit beneath \fIsha\fR and leave \fIsha\fR itself
above it, keeping its tree, message and identity \(em so
.B \-\-text
always names the inserted commit, and the surviving one keeps the original
subject whether or not it still fits.
.TP
.B \-\-reorder \fIcommit\fR...
Reorder a contiguous span; the positional arguments give the desired new
order, oldest\-first. Runs in an isolated worktree; the branch ref moves only
at the end. A conflict on the final step resolves itself \(em the reorder
preserves the tree, so the pre\-op blobs are the answer and the staged tree
proves it before anything is committed; an unprovable case pauses as usual.
A reorder only moves commits, so one that ends on another tree refuses to
land, naming the files \(em a resume whose resolutions changed them, and a
clean replay too, as a revert moved before what it reverts no longer takes
it back. Nor may a stop's resolution take in what a commit the new order puts
after it changes \(em a commit moved below one it builds on, its stop resolved
to what it holds, empties that one into it: the landing refuses, naming it,
and offers to squash the two instead \(em or, where other commits stood
between them, the move right after it. A stop whose pre\-op content would
