author date, which a reword keeps; a commit brought in from elsewhere replaces
nothing and lands as it was. It is the mark of a
rebuild loop reading a message with
.B %B
and handing it to
.B commit\-tree
unchanged, which adds a blank line to every message it rebuilds, one more
per pass, where neither
.B "git log"
nor
.B "git range\-diff"
shows it. A loop that carries a message byte for byte, or trims it, passes.
.IP
.B \-\-dry\-run
stops at the result: built in the same isolated worktree, it is reported
with the tip it was built on and a
.B "git range\-diff"
to compare the two, and left addressable by SHA with nothing verified or
applied. The last line before the trailer is its landing, pinned to that tip:
.BR "git edit \-\-exec \-\-base=<tip> \-\- git reset \-q \-\-hard <sha>" ,
which refuses if the branch moved since. Inspect a scripted rewrite this way
rather than by running the script outside
.BR \-\-exec ,
where the closing
.B "reset \-\-hard"
that hands its result back moves the shared checkout's branch and discards
its working tree.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-message
Also edit the commit message after applying changes \(em at a terminal. An
edit without one refuses it, as its pause amends from
.BR \-\-continue ,
which takes the message as
.B \-\-text
instead.
.TP
.B \-\-text \fImsg\fR
Inline message for
.BR \-M ,
.BR \-s / \-S ,
.BR \-\-split ,
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
or
.BR \-\-commit ,
which requires it
(skips the editor).
.B \-\-text \-
reads the message from standard input. With
.BR \-\-amend\-into
it must be given up front, not on
.BR \-\-continue :
the reword happens before the replay starts, so the commits being resolved
already carry the message. It replaces the whole message, not just the
subject: where a reword or a squash drops a body the new message does not
restate, the run names it and the commit it is still readable at \(em and
where a reword or an edit dropped the body whole for a subject alone, a
.B \-M \-\-text
command that, run as printed, puts it back under the new subject on the
landed commit, its blank lines kept bar trailing ones. A new message with a
body of its own gets no such command, which would drop that body.
.TP
.B \-\-subject \fIline\fR
With
.B \-M
or an edit pause's
.BR \-\-continue :
replace the message's subject alone \(em git's subject, the whole first
paragraph up to the first blank line, so
.B line one
and
.B line two
above a blank line are one subject \(em with \fIline\fR, keeping everything from
that blank line on byte for byte: body, trailers,
.B #
lines, line endings and a missing last newline. A message with no body becomes
\fIline\fR and a newline, as
.B \-\-text
ends one. \fIline\fR is trimmed as git trims a message; one holding a line
break, or nothing, refuses, as does
.B \-\-subject
beside
.B \-\-text
or with any other mode \(em one writing a new message, as
.BR \-\-commit ,
.BR \-\-split ,
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
.BR \-s / \-S ,
an implicit squash or a split's
.B \-\-continue
do, takes it whole as
.BR \-\-text .
Where the first paragraph it replaced held several lines, the run names them
and the commit they are still readable at, with the
.B \-\-text
form that keeps them. A subject the commit already has changes nothing:
.B \-M
says the message is unchanged, and a
.B \-\-continue
with no other change refuses, as with nothing to amend.
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-dir\fR[=\fIpath\fR]
Name the worktree an edit, drop or squash, or a land's replay, runs in (default:
.IR <repo> .git\-edit)
in place of a temporary one, which then stays after the run for the next to
reuse. A worktree that exists already is reused only detached and clean \(em one
holding a branch or tracked changes refuses, and under a gate so does one
holding untracked files, which the check would run over \(em in the default
path, which this tool manages, they are removed instead. It must be a worktree
of this repository, with no rebase, merge, cherry\-pick or revert in progress
outside the default path, and one run holds it at a time \(em a second refuses
while the first runs, as do a
.B \-\-continue
and an
.B \-\-abort
of the pause there while another run resumes or cancels it. A pause recorded
in that path as a run starts refuses the run once it holds the path, never
reset there, as does one recorded anywhere else in the repository meanwhile;
a path the run had just made goes again then, unless a pause there holds work.
An existing directory that is no worktree refuses \(em name one that does
not exist yet, which the run makes.
A lock whose
holder is gone \(em its process ended, or another started since under its
pid, while one another user runs counts as live \(em is broken through a hard
link to it, a break another run left half done waited out until its link is
10 s old; where the filesystem can't make one, the run refuses naming the
lock file, safe to delete once no run is live there.
A new one inside a working tree of this repository refuses unless
git ignores that path there, as the checkout's next
.B "git add \-A"
would stage it as an embedded repository. Only an
.B =
directly after the flag separates the value, and a short form runs on into
it:
.B \-Cy
names the path
.IR y .
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-pushed
Permit rewriting a commit that exists on a remote\-tracking ref. Refused by
default. A landing that takes the upstream's commits off the branch prints the
push that publishes it, its
.B \-\-force\-with\-lease
pinned to the upstream as the rewrite found it \(em a background fetch would
move the remote\-tracking ref under a bare lease, turning it into a plain force.
A
.B \-\-continue
or
.B \-\-undo
checks again at its landing \(em commits pushed while a pause waited, or since
the run an undo takes back, refuse there too unless it is given, a pause
keeping the run's own.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-new\-path
Let
.B \-\-amend\-into
fold a staged path into a commit that predates it, against the refusal
above under
.BR MODES .
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
Let
.B \-\-commit
take, or
.B \-\-amend\-into
fold, a file\-mode change \(em a flipped executable bit, or a link turned file \(em against the
refusal above under
.BR MODES ,
and on
.B \-\-continue
keep one a conflict resolution staged, or one the landing found nothing
asked for.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-other\-actor
Let
.B \-\-undo
take back a run, or
.BR \-\-continue ,
.B \-\-skip
and
.B \-\-abort
take on a pause, another
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
caller made \(em a parallel session's, or one of your own under another
label. A resume journals under the pause's own label.
.TP
.B \-\-tree=\fItree\-ish\fR
With
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
fold \fItree\-ish\fR's diff from
.B HEAD
instead of the index's \(em composed apart from the shared index, a commit
pinning the tip it was composed on, a bare tree checked against other
callers' landings; with
.BR \-\-commit ,
commit the tree of a commit composed on the tip; see
.BR MODES .
.TP
.B \-\-edits=\fIfile\fR|\fIjson\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
replay
.B {\(dq<path>\(dq: [[\(dq<old>\(dq, \(dq<new>\(dq], ...]}
onto the tip's text, given as a file or inline, each old text matching exactly
once, in order \(em one an earlier edit of the same file took its match from
refuses, naming that edit, as the two overlap and want merging into one, and a
fold of edits alone makes them itself at each stop of its replay whose commit
holds every old text once, pausing at the first that doesn't; see
.BR MODES .
The text is the one the commit stores: under a clean/smudge filter, an old text
read from the checkout holds the smudged form, refused as such, and
.B \-\-put
takes the whole file, cleaned as
.B "git add"
cleans it.
.TP
.B \-\-patch=\fIfile\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
apply a patch split by hand onto the tip \(em keep its context, no
.BR \-U0 .
It applies byte for byte, whatever
.B apply.whitespace
or
.B apply.ignoreWhitespace
say.
.TP
.B \-\-put=\fIpath\fR=\fIfile\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
replace \fIpath\fR with \fIfile\fR's content, checked against
.B \-\-base
where given, else against other callers' landings. A link put onto a link
stays one; elsewhere its content is read through, and a file put onto a link
is a type change
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
has to allow.
.TP
.B \-\-rm=\fIpath\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
remove \fIpath\fR, checked as
.B \-\-put
is.
.TP
.B \-\-chmod=\fIpath\fR=\fB+x\fR|\fB\-x\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
give \fIpath\fR that executable bit, whether or not it has it.
.TP
.B \-\-whole
With
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
fold each \fIpath\fR whole from the checkout, as
.B \-\-commit
takes it, rather than what is staged \(em alone or beside the inputs; see
.BR MODES .
.TP
.B \-\-snapshot
With
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
merge the fold into every first\-parent commit from its target up instead of
replaying the commits above it \(em each through
.B git merge\-tree
against the pre\-op tip as base, so a file moved since takes the fold under
its old name, and each commit keeps its author and message. A conflict stops
in a worktree laid out as any merge's, once per distinct content of the
conflicted file: its staged resolution serves every commit carrying the same,
and rerere pre\-fills a hunk already resolved at an earlier stop. Only a
conflict over content resolves this way \(em a rename or deletion meeting the
fold refuses, as does
.BR \-\-text .
A commit the fold leaves empty stays and is named. Needs git 2.40 or later.
.TP
.B \-\-base=\fIsha\fR
With
.BR \-\-exec ,
the tip its result was built on, as a SHA \(em a name like
.B HEAD
resolves to wherever the branch is now, the very tip the flag checks
against, so it refuses. Where the branch has moved past it, the run refuses
before the \fIcommand\fR starts, naming what reached the branch since and the
.B "git rebase \-\-onto"
that carries those onto the result inside the run; see
.BR MODES .
With
.BR \-\-commit ,
.B \-\-whole
or a fold of what is staged, and beside
.BR \-\-put ,
.B \-\-rm
or files taken whole beside the inputs,
the commit the files rest on: a path changed
between it and the tip refuses,
in place of the check against other callers' landings, and the tip's own SHA
takes a landing back on purpose, as for a fold of a bare
.BR \-\-tree .
Edits, patches, chmods and a
.B \-\-tree
commit composed on the tip apply to the tip itself, so
.B \-\-base
beside them alone refuses. With
.BR \-\-land ,
the commit \fIbranch\fR forked from, as a SHA in its history, over the fork
the reflogs name \(em the span is what \fIbranch\fR holds past it and the
target lacks.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
With
.BR \-\-exec ,
build and report the result, and the landing pinned to its base, without
verifying or applying it; with
.BR \-\-land ,
say whether it would fast\-forward or replay, and list the commits; see
.BR MODES .
Any verify flag refuses \(em
.BR \-\-verify ,
.BR \-\-verify\-span ,
.B \-\-no\-verify
or
.B \-\-no\-verify\-span
\(em since the gate, and any check skipped, belong to the run that lands the
result, decided once it has been inspected.
.TP
.B \-\-verify=\fIcmd\fR
Run \fIcmd\fR against what the rewrite built, before the compare\-and\-swap
applies it \(em by default at the commits the mode authored (a fold's amended
commit, an edit's), at the first commit it rebuilt, which a commit moved up or
dropped leaves missing what sat below it, at each commit a move or reorder puts
directly below one that came before it \(em the topmost commit moved down, or
the one a commit moved up lands on, where a commit it jumped over that needed
it still fails \(em at each commit a conflict resolution wrote, and at the new
tip, each
checked out in the operation's isolated worktree, with
.B GIT_EDIT_VERIFY_COMMIT
naming the commit under test. A resolution is checked where it was made
because git replayed the rest, and a later commit can heal a wrong one before
the tip ever shows it. On a terminal, or where
.B GIT_EDIT_PROGRESS
is set, each commit is named on a line of its own as its check starts \(em
.B "Checking 2 of 5 \(en <sha> <subject>"
\(em so a long gate can be followed. The command runs under git\-edit's own zsh,
which resets the options and aliases a caller's
.B .zshenv
sets, so an alias defined there does not resolve in it \(em a function or
a script does. The check runs against the linked
environment \(em the checkout's current dependencies \(em at every commit
alike, so each verdict compares like with like. A commit missing a file the command
references \(em any command token that names a path at the result tip
\(em is skipped with a note rather than failed, since the command could
only die there spuriously; a deep target predating the test runner thus
verifies what exists, and the tip always runs. A failure pauses the
operation unapplied, reporting what it can establish about it: the commit
the failing one replaces runs the same check, and where that fails too the
operation is cleared \(em the failure predates the rewrite, or the check
cannot run in that worktree at all; where it does not, the check climbs the commits above the failure
to the first one that passes \(em for a fold, the commit carrying what the
folded code now needs earlier. The climb is budgeted off the failing run's
own duration, which
.B edit.verifyBudget
overrides in seconds. The result is already built, so this is not a
content\-edit pause: nothing authored in that worktree can join the result,
so a resume refuses while any edit is present rather than check out over it
\(em repair the commit itself with a
.B git edit \fIcommit\fR
pass after the result applies. Files left untracked in the worktree feed the
check yet never land, so a resume with a gate refuses while it holds any that
are not ignored \(em bar an edit or content split being authored, which takes
them into the commit \(em and
.B \-\-exec
removes what its \fIcommand\fR left untracked before its gate runs, the
links standing in for ignored paths and so kept. What the check itself
leaves is removed after each run, so no run sees another's.
.B \-\-continue
re\-verifies \(em with the pause's own command and tier, or with what a
.B \-\-verify
or tier flag on the resume names, which then sticks to the operation as it
would on its first invocation \(em,
.B "\-\-no\-verify \-\-continue"
applies the result anyway, and
.B \-\-abort
cancels with nothing consumed \(em the built result is recorded, so a
resume restores the worktree to it if an inspect checkout of one of its
commits moved it, and refuses one on any other commit, an amend of the
result say.
Overrides
.BR edit.verifyCmd .
A reword,
.B \-S
and a squash routed to plumbing reuse every tree byte\-for\-byte and run no
verification \(em an explicit
.B \-\-verify
on one refuses rather than promise a gate the run never keeps. Every mode
that can change a tree does verify, including
.BR \-\-exec ,
whose command authors content nothing else has checked \(em the case a gate
is worth most on. A split verifies too \(em its extracted half is a tree no
commit ever carried, whatever the tip is \(em in a throwaway worktree.
Neither of those two pauses, so each refuses outright instead: a split
re\-derives its halves from its own worktree, and
.B \-\-exec
leaves its built commits addressable by SHA.
.TP
.B \-\-verify\-span
Verify every rebuilt commit instead of just the default tier's \(em
a green tip proves nothing about the span, so this is the tier for a
span\-wide rewrite. Needs a command from
.B \-\-verify
or
.BR edit.verifyCmd ,
and can stand for a repo through
.BR edit.verifySpan .
Given on
.BR \-\-continue ,
it raises a paused run to this tier for the rest of the operation, and
refuses up front where no command is in hand rather than record a tier no
resume could serve.
The default tier states how many of the commits it ran were resolved at a
stop and how many in between it left unchecked, so
the gap is visible where it matters rather than only in this page. The span
tier states in turn what it ran beyond the default one and what the whole
check took, since a caller picks its flags before a run, and the price that
reaches it in time is the previous run's.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify\-span
Where
.B edit.verifySpan
stands for the repo, step this one run back down to the default tier \(em the
commits the mode authored, the first one it rebuilt, each one a move or reorder
puts below a commit that came before it, the commits a conflict resolution
wrote, and the tip. A fold
or a move rebuilds every commit above its
target and the span tier checks each of them, which for a deep one\-line
change is the run's whole cost; this keeps the check at what the rewrite
authored, where
.B \-\-no\-verify
would skip it altogether. A pause keeps the declined tier for its resume, and
on
.B \-\-continue
the flag steps a recorded span down the same way. Refused together with
.BR \-\-verify\-span .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Skip a configured verification for this run \(em or, on
.BR \-\-continue ,
apply a rewrite whose verification failed.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Auto\-confirm the drop/squash confirmation prompt. A squash asks on either
route, plumbing or rebase, and only a terminal run asks at all \(em without a
TTY there is nobody to answer, so nothing prompts.
.TP
.B \-\-wait=\fIsecs\fR
How long a run waits for another caller's pause to clear before it refuses
(see
.I git\-edit\-state
under
.BR FILES ).
Without it a run waits up to 90 seconds, asking every 2 seconds, and then
runs on the tip the pause's landing left \(em or refuses, naming the pause's
owner and how long it waited. It prints one line as it starts waiting, naming
whose pause it is, its operation and how long it has stood, and one as the
pause clears; a pause another session takes in the meantime is waited out
within the same bound. A run waits only once its arguments passed every check,
so a bad one refuses at once, and it acts on the commits they named as it
started: a
.BR HEAD~1 ,
a branch name, a range or a
.B :/
search is pinned by SHA before the wait, and a commit the landing rewrote is
resolved as any stale SHA is \(em taken at its current identity where its
change is intact, else refused, naming its counterpart. An
.B \-\-onto
upstream naming a branch is taken as that branch stands once the wait is
over, and any other, such as
.B main~1
or
.BR main@{1} ,
as it stood when the run began \(em one on the branch's own line that the
landing rewrote resolved as a stale SHA is, so the branch is never replanted
onto the orphaned commit.
.B \-\-wait=0
refuses at once, leaving another caller's pause to that caller or to a run
with a longer
.BR \-\-wait ,
while an unlabeled caller, which may take up any pause, is told to continue
or abort one that is its own.
A pause of the caller's own \(em the same
.BR GIT_EDIT_ACTOR ,
or neither labeled \(em is never waited for, as only its
.B \-\-continue
or
.B \-\-abort
ends it, nor one an older git\-edit wrote before pauses recorded their
label, which any caller may resume. Only the run's first check waits,
before it takes any lock or worktree; one asked again later, a pause
recorded while the run went on, refuses as before. Ctrl\-C or
.B SIGTERM
while waiting stops the run with nothing taken. For a longer wait, say
.BR \-\-wait=600 ,
run git\-edit in the background, or in the foreground under a 10\-minute call
timeout. Refused beside
.BR \-\-continue ,
.B \-\-skip
and
.BR \-\-abort ,
which act on the pause itself, and beside the standalone commands.
.TP
.B \-\-skip
With
.B \-\-onto
or
.BR \-\-land ,
resume past the paused commit instead of through it \(em for one the upstream
already carries in another form, where resolving would duplicate it. Refused
for every other operation, whose paused commit is the point of the run.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage block. Prefer it to
.B \-\-help
in scripts and agent runs:
.BR git (1)
intercepts
.B \-\-help
for subcommands and renders this page through
.BR man ,
which without a terminal emits backspace\-overstruck text several times the
size of the usage block.
.SH "STANDALONE COMMANDS"
.TP
.B \-\-continue
Resume the paused operation: after staging the resolution in the printed
worktree for a conflict, or after authoring there for an edit or content
split. It takes
.BR \-\-text ,
an edit's
.BR \-\-subject ,
the verify flags, the allow flags and
.B \-y
\(em a \fIcommit\fR or a mode beside it, or beside
.BR \-\-skip ,
refuses, naming what it got, rather than resume and leave that undone.
It decides on the pause as it stands once it holds the pause's worktree: one
another resume took to a later stop meanwhile refuses, the pause kept for
the next.
.TP
.B \-\-abort
Cancel a paused operation and roll back. The branch was never moved and
staged changes were never consumed, so there is nothing to undo. A
\fIcommit\fR or a mode beside it refuses the same way, while what a resume
takes is let through, so the way out never blocks. It changes nothing while a
resume of the pause runs \(em a resume holds the pause's worktree, as a second
.B \-\-continue
finds too \(em or a landing holds the journal past 60 s, and says so where a
resume landed the operation meanwhile. A resume that died holds nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-undo
Revert the last completed operation's ref move, compare\-and\-swap guarded
(refused if the branch has moved on since). The journal is the repository's,
so its last operation can be a parallel session's on any branch: with
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
set, a run made under another label, or none, is refused too. A run whose
branch already sits where the undo would take it \(em moved back by hand, with
the raw
.B "git update\-ref"
a refusal names, say \(em moves nothing: it says so, ends
.BR "ok \(en \fIref\fP unchanged, already at \fIsha\fP" ,
and records the run as undone, so a further
.B \-\-undo
takes back the one before it. A line repeating the branch, old and new tip
of a later one an undo took back \(em an older git\-edit journaling a move
another run had journaled from the reflog \(em is passed over the same way.
The checkout follows as after any landing \(em
but for what a
.BR \-\-commit ,
a fold or an
.B \-\-exec
that only added commits landed, which may have come from the checkout and so
stays there as uncommitted changes, only the index entries re\-synced; an
.B \-\-exec
that rewrote history is undone as any rewrite is. The raw
.B "git update\-ref"
every landing prints beside
.B "git edit \-\-undo"
moves the ref alone, the checkout left as it is: the index entries the run
moved are named with the step that re\-syncs them back, the index alone,
and, where
.B \-\-undo
would bring the files back, the
.B \-\-carry
that does it after \(em a
.B "git restore \-\-staged"
of exactly those paths, a no\-op before that undo, guarded by a
.B "git diff\-index \-\-cached \-\-exit\-code"
that resets none where one holds other staging since, a peer's say, and lists
those. Where one of those paths is flagged skip\-worktree or lies outside a
sparse checkout \(em any path, on a git before 2.42, which can't ask its
patterns \(em a restore would miss it or clear its flag, so no such step is
printed: the run says the raw form moves the ref alone and names
.BR "git edit \-\-undo" ,
which re\-syncs them, their flags kept.
.TP
.BR \-\-carry [ =\fIold\-tip\fR ]
Merge the checkout's uncommitted edits onto what a rewrite landed, for each
path it changed whose file holds edits on the pre\-rewrite content \(em the
files a landing that could not bring the checkout along names, or a sync's
conflict left, as taking the rewrite back if committed whole \(em and
move a renamed file's edits to its new path, also where the checkout holds
that path exactly as the rewrite landed it, or as the old one's own file \(em a
case\-only rename on a filesystem that ignores case. A renamed file still
unedited at its old path moves too, taking what landed, so one run brings the
checkout to the new layout. Where the rewrite renamed a directory whole \(em
every path it tracked gone to the same place under one new directory, as git's
own directory renames read \(em the untracked files left in it move to the
same place under the new one, never over a file already there, which is named
and left; ignored files stay, the directory's own
.I .gitignore
counting, and nothing under a symlink is touched. A
directory renamed only in part takes no untracked file along, the ones right in
it named as left where they are, and one renamed by case alone, on a filesystem
that ignores case, takes the new spelling. Every other path the rewrite changed
is
brought along as a terminal run's sync brings it: a file whose content is
exactly what the old tip had takes what is at the tip, keeping a mode the
checkout changed, one the rewrite removed goes where its content is exactly
the old blob \(em first, so a directory the rewrite turned into a file makes
way for it \(em and one it added that the checkout lacks is written \(em each
index entry still as the old tip had it, or missing for an added one, taking
what landed, also where the file holds what landed already or was carried
before, while a file deleted in the checkout stays deleted and untracked files
stay. An intent\-to\-add entry where the rewrite adds the file takes what
landed, its file left as it is. A merged file takes the mode that landed,
unless the checkout changed its own \(em under
.B core.fileMode=false
as its index entry has it, never by the executable bit. Where the rewrite
changed a
.IR .gitattributes ,
every side merges under the attributes the edits were made under \(em from
git 2.40, as an older one merges under the new ones, where a changed
.B eol
reads as a conflict, the edits left as they were. One run
leaves the checkout at what landed, the caller's edits merged on top. The
rewrite is the
branch's last one on record, a
.B \-\-commit
landing aside, or the one from \fIold\-tip\fR to \fBHEAD\fR. Only a clean merge
that keeps the edits' own lines whole is written, and only over the file as it
was read. Nothing is written through a symlink, wherever it points, or outside
a sparse checkout \(em one with no patterns file being full, as git reads
it \(em where an entry alone takes what landed, its skip\-worktree
