.SH "CONFLICT RESOLUTION"
When a rebase\- or autosquash\-based mode hits a conflict it pauses (exit
status 2), prints the temporary worktree, the conflicted files, the failing
step, and the steps that remain, and emits a
.B conflict
trailer. Every remaining step that touches a conflicted file is named under
that file: each reapplies its own change on top, so the resolution is the
content before they replay, never their result folded in early.
Resolve the files in that worktree, stage them, and run
.B git edit \-\-continue
(repeat if the conflict cascades), or
.B git edit \-\-abort
to cancel. A pause with no conflicted files means the current step became
empty (its changes are already upstream);
.B git edit \-\-continue
drops that commit, as does a resolution staged empty. A commit lost by hand
instead \(em taken out of the rebase's todo, or stepped over with a raw
.B "git rebase \-\-skip"
whose change the replay does not already hold \(em refuses the resume, named,
landing nothing, and
.B \-\-status
gives the same verdict:
.B git edit \-\-skip
is the way to leave one out of a replant or a land, and elsewhere the run
starts again. Before git 2.40 whether the replay holds that change is read
path by path, following no rename, so one held only under a renamed path,
or in a link or a binary changed beside it, refuses too.
A change left unstaged at a pause with no conflicted files refuses the
continue instead, as git resumes
from staged content only \(em the pause names those files, with the
.B "git add"
and
.B "git restore"
that clear it.
.PP
A file whose committed bytes its attributes would store otherwise \(em one
committed with CRLF line ends under
.BR eol=lf ,
say \(em reads as modified wherever git has just written it, though it is
nobody's change. A run settles such a file in its worktrees as it starts,
