.SH SCRIPTING
Notes for driving
.B git edit
from a script or an agent loop \(em what the machine\-readable surface
guarantees, and the loop that consumes it safely.
.SS Reading a run
Dispatch on the trailer (see
.BR "OUTCOME TRAILER" ),
not on an exit status a pipeline may have replaced with its last command's.
And keep more than the trailer: under
.B "| tail \-3"
only the trailer and the touched\-commit line above it hold their positions
\(em the summary lines above those (an edit's net history change, a fold's
commits a replay dropped as empty, counted and named, a tip differing from the staged
result) grow with the commit, and they are where a rewrite reports what it
did to the rest of history. A wrapper that forwards only the last line
drops exactly those findings.
.SS The resolve loop
The safe shape for resolving a
.B conflict
pause externally:
.IP 1. 4
Take the worktree path from the trailer, and list what needs resolving from
the index \(em
.B "git \-C <worktree> \-c core.quotePath=false diff \-\-name\-only \-\-diff\-filter=U"
\(em which names a non\-ASCII file as
.B git add
takes it \(em never by grepping for markers (see the pause states below).
.IP 2. 4
Resolve one file at a time, and validate each result before staging it:
parse it, run the test that covers it, compare its row or line count against
both parents. \(lqNo markers left\(rq is not validation \(em
.B \-\-continue
refuses staged
.B <<<<<<<
blocks on its own, but semantically wrong content passes every marker check.
.B \-\-verify
runs this class of check for you, against the whole built result before it
applies (see
.BR OPTIONS ).
.IP 3. 4
Stage each file singly
.RB ( "git \-C <worktree> add <file>" ),
never a blanket
.B "add \-A"
chained into
.B \-\-continue
in one command \(em that stages whatever a broken resolver left behind
before anything could look at it.
.IP 4. 4
.B "git edit \-\-continue"
and dispatch on the fresh trailer; a cascade repeats the loop.
.SS Pauses without markers
A marker grep conflates pause states that need different responses \(em
dispatch on the trailer first, then on the unmerged list from step 1:
.IP \(bu 3
A
.B paused
trailer naming a verify failure: nothing is unmerged and nothing is to be
resolved \(em the result is built and the caller's own check rejected it.
Editing that worktree is refused rather than folded in; apply the result with
.B "\-\-no\-verify \-\-continue"
and repair the named commit with its own
.B git edit
pass, or
.BR \-\-abort .
.IP \(bu 3
A
.B conflict
trailer with no unmerged files at all: the current step became empty \(em the
skip case under
.BR "CONFLICT RESOLUTION" ,
nothing to resolve.
.IP \(bu 3
An unmerged add/add or modify/delete: git may place one side's file whole
(or none at all), with no textual merge to mark \(em the file reads as
finished and is not.
.IP \(bu 3
A recorded resolution replayed by
.BR rerere :
the file arrives pre\-filled and merely needs staging \(em but validate it
first (below).
.PP
The pause output flags such files itself, with a
.B "No conflict markers in"
line naming them.
.SS Recorded resolutions
.B rerere
replays a recorded resolution into every later conflict with the same
preimage \(em a cascade step, a retry, an unrelated future rewrite over the
same lines \(em marker\-free. Step 2's validation applies to a pre\-filled
file exactly as to a fresh resolution: a wrong resolution, recorded once,
replays wrong every time after, and nothing flags it.
.SS Span campaigns
A mechanical change to many commits \(em a format migration, a normalizer
sweep \(em should not run as one fold or edit per commit: each replays every
descendant as a diff and re\-derives the same conflicts. Map snapshots
instead, inside
.BR \-\-exec :
.PP
.nf
.RS
git edit \-\-exec \-\- zsh \-c '
  NEW=<base>
  for C in $(git rev\-list \-\-reverse <base>..HEAD); do
    git checkout \-qf $C && git clean \-qfd
    transform .          # the pure per\-snapshot rewrite
    git add \-A
    NEW=$(git commit\-tree $(git write\-tree) \-p $NEW \\
          \-m "$(git log \-1 \-\-format=%B $C)")
  done
  git reset \-q \-\-hard $NEW'
.RE
.fi
.PP
The transform applies to each commit's snapshot, so no step can conflict,
and because the script ends with HEAD moved,
.B \-\-exec
supplies the compare\-and\-swap, the pushed guard, the journal entry and the
gate around it \(em though where
.B edit.verifySpan
stands for the repo, that gate runs once per commit the sweep built, so a long
one wants
.BR \-\-no\-verify\-span ,
a cheaper
.B \-\-verify
or
.BR \-\-no\-verify .
This is a sketch: a real loop also carries each commit's author and author
date onto
.B commit\-tree
.RB ( GIT_AUTHOR_NAME / EMAIL / DATE )
and its message as the shell's
.B $(...)
reads it, trailing newlines trimmed, or byte for byte from
.B "git cat\-file commit"
\(em ported to a language that keeps them,
.B %B
gains a blank line per pass, which the landing refuses. It leaves
the committer date to the run, since a result carrying commits
dated before it lands only under
.BR \-\-base ,
and stops at merge commits rather than flattening them. Conflict\-freedom
cuts the other way too: a buggy transform lands on every commit uniformly
and silently, so sweep the result before trusting it \(em run the tests per
rebuilt commit, or diff each rebuilt tree against its source.
.B \-\-dry\-run
leaves the result unapplied for that sweep and prints the command that
lands it.
.PP
A hand\-authored fold stays on
.B \-\-amend\-into
while its replay runs clean. Where later commits rewrote its lines, the
replay stops at each of them for a state written by hand. Then
.B \-\-abort
and fold with
.B \-\-snapshot
instead, which stops once per distinct content of the file, or restate the fold
as
.B \-\-edits
that every commit from its target up holds exactly once, whose replay resolves
each of those stops itself. Prove the tip against the result intended,
and read each patch the range\-diff marks changed beyond the targets rather than
counting them: each is a later commit the fold's text reached.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success (including a no\-op that left the ref unchanged).
.TP
.B 1
Error \(em nothing was applied, bar what
.B \-\-carry
names as carried before the paths it left. Every mode but
.BR \-\-version ,
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-selftest
refuses so outside a repository, and from a current directory that is gone
\(em a pause worktree its own
.B \-\-continue
removed, say \(em naming which.
.TP
.B 2
Paused \(em on a conflict, for an edit or content split, or at a failed
verify or mode gate \(em awaiting
.B \-\-continue
or
.BR \-\-abort .
.TP
.I other
.B \-\-exec
exits with its \fIcommand\fR's own failing status, so a command exiting 2
or 130 returns that too \(em the trailer, an
.BR error ,
tells them apart.
.TP
.BR 129 ", " 130 ", " 143
Stopped by SIGHUP, SIGINT or SIGTERM before the branch moved, with an
.B error
trailer naming the signal. SIGPIPE stops no run: whatever reads the output
going away \(em a GUI quitting mid\-run, a pipe into
.B head
\(em costs only the output from there, the run landing, pausing or refusing
as it would and cleaning up after. The run cleans up as a failed one does, its
worktree removed unless a pause it saved still points there, so a
.B \-\-continue
stopped mid\-check stays paused for another. A signal sent to git\-edit alone
takes effect once the check its gate is running returns, while one sent to the
whole process group, as a terminal's Ctrl\-C is, ends that check too.
.IP
Once the branch's
.B update\-ref
has begun, the run finishes instead \(em the journal entry
.B \-\-undo
reads, the rewrite delivery and the index re\-sync all still happen \(em and
exits 0, a line above its
.B ok
trailer naming the signal. A second SIGHUP, SIGINT or SIGTERM stops it from
there \(em one arriving while the
.B update\-ref
runs once it is through \(em with a trailer naming the move \(em
.B "stopped by SIGTERM after refs/heads/main moved <old-sha> \(-> <new-sha>"
\(em that
.B "git edit \-\-undo"
takes back. It re\-syncs the index entries the move stranded first, as its
landing would have \(em left on the old tip, they read as a staged revert of
the move that a peer's plain
.B "git commit"
takes in \(em the index alone, and the trailer names them; where the index is
locked, it names the guarded
.B "git diff \-\-cached \-\-exit\-code"
and
.B "git restore \-\-staged"
that re\-sync them once it is free. One sent to the whole process group still
ends the Git command or
hook running at that moment, so the run reads again what that command was
reading, and redoes a re\-sync or report it cut short, rather than take its
cut\-short answer for \(lqnothing changed\(rq; one that ends the
.B update\-ref
itself before the branch moved stops the run as that signal.
.IP
An
.B \-\-abort
finishes likewise once it has cleared the pause \(em its rebase aborted and its
worktree removed, a line above its
.B ok
trailer naming the signal \(em and a second signal stops it with
.B "stopped by SIGTERM after cancelling the pause (<operation> <sha>)"
the worktree removed all the same. A signal at a terminal's prompt leaves the
pause as q does, exiting 2, and one arriving once the trailer is out exits with
the status the trailer stands for. A run stopped while it holds a pause it
saved or took up, and has not landed, keeps it, worktree and all \(em one
arriving as the pause is first saved included \(em its trailer adding
.BR "its pause stays, \(aqgit edit \-\-status\(aq shows it" .
.IP
A run killed outright (SIGKILL) once its branch moved leaves the move in the
branch's reflog alone. The next run on that branch, its
.B \-\-status
or
.B \-\-undo
included, journals it from there \(em under the label and caller its reflog
message names, so
.B \-\-undo
takes it back and the whole\-file guards weigh it \(em and re\-syncs the index
entries it stranded, saying so in one line. It does so only where no other
run holds the journal's lock, never waiting for one, and never for an
.B undo
move, which
.B \-\-undo
already finds back where it went \(em and only where the run that died held
that lock, as a run of 1.8.0 holds it across its move, or once the entry is
15 s old: a git\-edit before 1.8.0 moves the branch first and journals it a
moment later, up to 5 s where it waits for the lock, so a younger move may be
a live run's. Killed once its move is journaled but
before its index re\-sync, a run leaves that re\-sync to the next run the
same way: each landing marks its re\-sync owed, then done, in a
.I git\-edit\-resynced
file in the checkout's git dir, and a journaled move at the tip it names as
owed by a run that is gone is re\-synced once, in one line \(em as is one
the mark doesn't name where it names the move before it on that branch and
the run that died held the journal's lock. A mark naming another branch, a
switch in that checkout, or none, proves nothing, so a revert staged by hand
after a landing that finished is never touched, nor after a move by a
git\-edit before 1.8.0, which marks nothing and re\-synced its own.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B edit.worktreeLink
Gitignored paths to symlink from the checkout into every temporary worktree,
one per value (repeatable). A pause exists so the commit can be verified in
isolation, but a project's own checks usually need what the repository
deliberately does not track \(em a
.IR node_modules ,
a virtualenv \(em and a fresh worktree has none of it. Links rather than
copies, so a check there cannot diverge from what the real checkout has
installed. A pattern written for a directory
.RI ( node_modules/ )
does not match the symlink that stands in for it, so such a link would land
untracked where the directory was ignored \(em and the amend at
.B \-\-continue
stages the worktree wholesale. That link is therefore not created, and the
reason is reported. Not creating it is the whole remedy: an ignored link can
never be staged, so nothing has to be removed afterwards.
.IP
Link only what is regenerable \(em never the root of irreplaceable shared
data. A symlinked root is followed by anything that walks it: on BSD/macOS,
.B "rm \-rf <link>/"
with a trailing slash resolves through it and deletes the shared tree
behind it. For a corpus or fixtures tree, name the specific subdirectory
.RI ( data/corpus
rather than
.IR data )
\(em nested values work, and their parent directories are created in the
worktree. A value added while an operation is paused reaches the existing
worktree on \fB\-\-continue\fR. A value the checkout has nothing at is named
rather than skipped silently, as a check needing it fails on that alone. So
is one below a path the commit itself tracks as a symlink or a file: the
commit's entry stays, and a link through it would land outside the worktree.
.TP
.B edit.verifyCmd
Standing verification command, applied as if every operation passed
.B \-\-verify=\fIcmd\fR
\(em the check a rewrite must pass before it lands. Pair it with
.B edit.worktreeLink
so the isolated worktree holds what the check needs to run.
.TP
.B edit.verifySpan
Boolean. Runs every verification at the span tier, as if each operation
passed
.BR \-\-verify\-span .
For a repo whose history is kept green commit by commit \(em where it is
not, the tier reports breakage the rewrite never caused. It names a tier and
not a check, so with no command configured it raises nothing rather than
refusing the run. Each run then reports what the tier cost beyond the default
one, and
.B \-\-no\-verify\-span
steps a single run back down.
.TP
.B edit.verifyBudget
Seconds the post\-failure climb for the first passing commit may spend, zero
to skip it altogether. Unset, it is five times the failing run's own duration
(at least 30s), so a slow check stops early on its own.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Set to
.BR 0 ,
.BR false ,
.B no
or
.BR off ,
a
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_*
switch or
.B GIT_EDIT_PROGRESS
counts as unset.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
A label for whoever runs git\-edit \(em an agent session's id, a CI job \(em
closing every reflog message it writes as \fB [\fR\fIlabel\fR\fB]\fR, so a
history viewer can name who moved the branch. The reflog never leaves the
clone, so neither does the label. Any character but letters, digits,
.B _
and
.B \-
maps to
.BR _ ,
since it could break the command the message rides in, and the run says so,
as two labels may then read as one there. Whose run or pause it is goes by
the label as given, though: the journal and a pause record a mapped one in
\fB$\(aq\&...\(aq\fR quoting, so two labels mapping alike stay two callers \(em a pause or
journal line recorded before git\-edit kept it so holds the mapped label
alone, and counts as the caller's whose label maps to it, for
.B \-\-undo
too. A set label marks the run as an agent's:
no prompt at a pause, no checkout brought along, at a terminal too. An undo's
entry names only whoever undid; a pause records its label, which another
label's
.BR \-\-continue ,
.B \-\-skip
or
.B \-\-abort
refuses, as it does a state file cut short to where it names neither its
label nor its operation. The
journal records the label too, for
.B \-\-undo
to refuse a labeled caller another's run, naming the caller's own last run
and, where its branch still stands on it, the
.B "git update\-ref"
its landing printed, which still takes it back \(em and for the
.BR \-\-commit / \-\-whole
landing guard to tell another caller's landing from the caller's own: runs
under the caller's label never count, so for an unlabeled caller every
unlabeled run is its own, and a file may take any of them back.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_WORKTREE_LINK
Colon\-separated paths to link into temporary worktrees, added to whatever
.B edit.worktreeLink
already names.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set (or if stdout is not a terminal, or
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb ),
output is emitted without ANSI escapes \(em so captured runs stay greppable
as plain text.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_RESOLVE
If set, disables resolving a stale \fIcommit\fR to its post\-rewrite identity;
every unreachable commit is then simply refused.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_AUTO_OPEN
If set, does not auto\-open the worktree in a detected GUI editor at
interactive pauses.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_REPLAY
If set, the plumbing modes rebuild descendants one \fBcommit\-tree\fR at a time
instead of in one proven \fBgit replay\fR pass.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_PROGRESS
If set, the verify gate names each commit as its check starts even where
stdout is not a terminal \(em for a caller that shows a long gate's progress.
A captured run leaves the lines out otherwise, since nobody watches them live.
.TP
.BR GIT_DIR ", " GIT_WORK_TREE ", " GIT_INDEX_FILE ", " GIT_COMMON_DIR ", " GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY
Taken, and dropped, only where they name what the directory finds without
them; otherwise refused, as every git the run's temporary worktrees start would
take them too and work on the caller's checkout. A work tree only
.B GIT_DIR
reaches, as in a dotfiles setup, is told the
.B .git
file that works instead \(em a
.B gitdir:
line naming that repository, as
.B "git init \-\-separate\-git\-dir"
writes, with
.B core.bare
set false where it was true. The file goes at the work tree's top, a path
named only where it is known \(em from
.B GIT_WORK_TREE
or
.BR core.worktree ,
or the current directory where every tracked file is found from it \(em as
git takes the current directory for the top otherwise, and a
.B .git
file in a subdirectory would have every tracked file read as deleted.
.SH FILES
All three sit in the repository's common git directory, so every worktree
shares them.
.TP
.I <git dir>/git\-edit\-state
The paused operation \(em its worktree, branch, target and phase. Only one can
be paused at a time: while this file exists every mutating mode waits for it
to clear, up to
.B \-\-wait
(90 seconds by default), then refuses rather
than move the branch under a resolution that has not applied yet, whose own
compare\-and\-swap would then fail at the end \(em
.BR \-\-commit ,
.B \-\-exec
and
.B \-\-undo
only where the pause is on the branch they move, and
.B \-\-undo
refuses at once, naming whose pause it is \(em another caller's is left to
that caller to continue or abort \(em as does a run on a pause of the caller's
own. A run
reaching a pause of its
own after a parallel session's took the slot refuses without waiting, and
removes
the temporary worktree it made, as nothing there is authored yet. An edit,
drop or squash asks again once it holds its
.B \-C
path, or before it makes its temporary worktree, so a pause recorded while it
waited or prompted refuses it there, at once \(em waiting while holding a path
would block it for others.
.B \-\-continue
and
.B \-\-abort
clear it.
.TP
.I <git dir>/git\-edit\-state\-verify
The failing verification's whole output, headed by the command, its exit status
and its duration \(em the pause itself prints only the last 15 lines, and the
trailer names this path for a driver reading through a
.BR "| tail -N" .
It lives as long as the pause.
.TP
.I <git dir>/git\-edit\-journal
One line per completed ref move (\fB<epoch> <ref> <old> <new> <label>\fR,
then a tab and the caller's
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
label as given for a labeled run, in \fB$\(aq\&...\(aq\fR quoting where it
maps), behind
.B \-\-undo
and the last\-operation report of
.BR \-\-status .
Its lock,
.IR git\-edit\-journal.lock ,
held from a branch's
.B update\-ref
until its line is in, and across each write to a pause, names the run holding
it by pid and start time. Another run waits while that run lives, up to 60 s,
then refuses, moving nothing \(em at once where it runs under that run, as a
.B git edit
a landing's hook runs does; one whose run is gone is broken at once, and one
naming none, as a run killed while writing it leaves, once older than 10 s
\(em and one naming a pid alone, as an older git\-edit wrote it, once older
than 60 s, its pid then taken for another process's. A run that cannot read
its own start time names it
.BR \- ,
held for as long as its pid lives.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Fold staged changes into the commit that introduced the code:
.B git add src/foo.js && git edit \-\-amend\-into=auto
.TP
Reword an earlier commit without touching the working tree:
.B git edit \-M \-\-text="Better subject" abc1234
.TP
Change only its subject, the body kept byte for byte:
.B git edit \-M \-\-subject="Better subject" abc1234
.TP
Split an icons change out of a mixed commit:
.B git edit \-\-split=abc1234 \-\-text="Add icons" \-\- src/svg/
.TP
Split a refactor out of a commit that mixed it into one file (\fB\-\-text\fR names the lower commit):
.B git edit \-\-split=abc1234 \-\-text="Refactor: Rename helpers"
.TP
Land a finished branch into a checkout others' uncommitted work sits in:
.B git edit \-\-land=feature \-\-dry\-run && git edit \-\-land=feature
.TP
Fold with the test suite as the gate:
.B git edit \-\-amend\-into=abc1234 \-\-verify='npm test' \-\- src/foo.js
.TP
Land a branch into a checkout holding uncommitted edits, then bring them
along from the old tip the land names \(em a bare \fB\-\-carry\fR takes
whichever rewrite landed last, another session's too:
.B git edit \-\-land=topic
then
.B git edit \-\-carry=<old tip>
.TP
Undo the last operation:
.B git edit \-\-undo
